//Rule of 3
The Rule of 3 is a C++ rule for classes that manage resources manually, such as dynamically allocated memory.

If a class needs to define any one of these three:

Destructor
Copy Constructor
Copy Assignment Operator

then it will often need to define all three.

Why?

Suppose your class has:

int* data;

and manages memory using new.

Then you need to properly handle:

Copy Constructor
      ↓
Copy Assignment
      ↓
Destructor

Otherwise, you can get problems like:

shallow copies
double deletion
memory leaks
Example
class A {
public:
    int* data;

    A(int x) {
        data = new int(x);
    }

    A(const A& a) {
        data = new int(*a.data);
    }

    A& operator=(const A& a) {
        if (this != &a) {
            delete data;
            data = new int(*a.data);
        }
        return *this;
    }

    ~A() {
        delete data;
    }
};

These are the 3:

1. Copy Constructor
2. Copy Assignment Operator
3. Destructor

Simple definition:

Rule of 3 = if a resource-managing class needs one of destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment, it generally needs all three.